    "public_path": "/en/pork-bondiola-recipe",
    "recipe_group_id": "af4f77b1-24cd-4c6f-8d02-dbc92e3cfc24",
    "expected_updated_at": "2026-10-25T04:48:08.371477+00:00",
    "before": {
      "title": "Oven-Roasted Pork Neck (Argentine Bondiola)",
      "excerpt": "Oven-roasted pork neck (bondiola) with a mustard, honey, garlic and soy marinade. Slow covered cooking, resting time and a pan-juice sauce.",
      "summary": "Bondiola is the pork neck, a cut with fat marbling that turns tender with slow cooking. In Argentina and Uruguay it's a weekend table classic, and in other countries it's sold as pork collar or pork neck. This recipe makes it in a home oven with a marinade of mustard, honey, garlic and soy sauce that caramelizes on the surface.\n\nThe secret is three steps: browning the piece before roasting it, cooking it covered with a little water so it softens, and letting it rest before slicing. The pan juices become a simple sauce to spoon over the slices, served with roast potatoes or chimichurri.",
      "notes": "<h3>Tips</h3><ul><li>Bondiola has a lot of connective tissue: the safe minimum for pork is 145°F (63°C) with a 3-minute rest, but for a tender result you need to reach 176–185°F (80–85°C). A meat thermometer is the reliable way to check.</li><li>Time depends on thickness: a 1 kg (2.2 lb) piece needs about 1 hour 40 minutes covered; a 2 kg (4.4 lb) piece, about 2 hours.</li><li>Always slice against the grain with a sharp knife.</li><li>For pulled bondiola, leave it in the oven until 190–194°F (88–90°C) and shred with two forks.</li></ul><h3>Substitutions</h3><ul><li>No Dijon mustard: yellow mustard, in a smaller amount.</li><li>No honey: brown sugar or maple syrup, same amount.</li><li>No soy sauce: an extra teaspoon of salt plus a splash of broth.</li><li>In some countries this cut is sold as pork collar or pork neck.</li></ul><h3>Storage</h3><p>Refrigerate the meat and juices within 2 hours of taking it out of the oven, in closed containers. It keeps for 3 to 4 days and reheats gently with some of the sauce to 165°F (74°C). You can freeze it in portions for up to 3 months.</p><p class='md-sources'>Sources: <a href='https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/safe-temperature-chart' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>USDA FSIS, minimum safe temperatures</a>; <a href='https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/leftovers-and-food-safety' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>USDA FSIS, leftovers and food safety</a>.</p>",
      "steps": [
        {
          "title": "Pat dry and season the pork",
          "content": "Take the piece out of the fridge 30 minutes before starting. Pat it dry on all sides with paper towels and season with the salt and pepper. A dry surface browns better and the marinade sticks instead of sliding off.",
          "image_alt": "Raw pork neck patted dry and seasoned on a cutting board",
          "image_url": "/recetas/bondiola-de-cerdo/paso-01.webp"
        },
        {
          "title": "Make the marinade",
          "content": "In a bowl, mix the chopped garlic, Dijon mustard, honey, soy sauce, olive oil, lemon juice and sweet paprika into a thick, even paste.",
          "image_alt": "Mustard, honey, garlic, soy sauce and paprika marinade in a bowl",
          "image_url": "/recetas/bondiola-de-cerdo/paso-02.webp"
        },
        {
          "title": "Marinate and let it rest",
          "content": "Coat the piece on all sides with the marinade, using a brush or clean hands. Cover and refrigerate for at least 1 hour; if you can, leave it overnight so the garlic and salt penetrate.",
          "image_alt": "Pork neck coated in marinade, ready to rest in the fridge",
          "image_url": "/recetas/bondiola-de-cerdo/paso-03.webp"
        },
        {
          "title": "Brown the pork",
          "content": "Preheat the oven to 320°F (160°C) with the rack in the center. Heat a large skillet over medium-high heat and brown the piece for 3 to 4 minutes per side, until the surface is toasted. The honey darkens fast: watch that it doesn't burn.",
          "image_alt": "Pork neck browning in a cast-iron skillet",
          "image_url": "/recetas/bondiola-de-cerdo/paso-04.webp"
        },
        {
          "title": "Roast covered for 2 hours",
          "content": "Transfer the piece to a roasting pan with 200 ml of water in the bottom and cover with foil. Roast at 320°F (160°C) for 2 hours: slow, moist cooking softens the cut's connective tissue.",
          "image_alt": "Pork neck covered with foil inside the oven",
          "image_url": "/recetas/bondiola-de-cerdo/paso-05.webp"
        },
        {
          "title": "Uncover and caramelize",
          "content": "Remove the foil, raise the oven to 392°F (200°C) and roast 15 to 20 minutes more, until the surface is dark and glossy. Check the thickest part with a thermometer: for a tender result it should read 176–185°F (80–85°C) (the safe minimum is 145°F/63°C).",
          "image_alt": "Caramelized pork neck with a thermometer inserted in the center",
          "image_url": "/recetas/bondiola-de-cerdo/paso-06.webp"
        },
        {
          "title": "Let it rest for 15 minutes",
          "content": "Move the pork to a board and cover loosely with foil. Let it rest for 15 minutes: the juices redistribute instead of running out at the first cut.",
          "image_alt": "Pork neck resting on a cutting board before slicing",
          "image_url": "/recetas/bondiola-de-cerdo/paso-07.webp"
        },
        {
          "title": "Make the pan sauce",
          "content": "Strain the pan juices into a saucepan and skim off excess fat from the surface. Boil over medium heat for 5 to 8 minutes, scraping the bottom, until it thickens slightly. Taste and adjust the salt.",
          "image_alt": "Pan juices being reduced into a sauce",
          "image_url": "/recetas/bondiola-de-cerdo/paso-08.webp"
        },
        {
          "title": "Slice and serve",
          "content": "Slice the pork into ½-inch (1.5 cm) rounds, against the grain. Serve hot with the reduced pan juices on top, roast potatoes and chimichurri or Argentine salad.",
          "image_alt": "Sliced pork neck with pan juices, roast potatoes and chimichurri",
          "image_url": "/recetas/bondiola-de-cerdo/paso-09.webp"
        }
      ],
