    "after": {
      "title": "Juicy Oven-Roasted Pork Neck (Bondiola), Foil-Covered",
      "excerpt": "Oven-roasted pork neck (bondiola) with a mustard, honey, garlic and soy marinade. Slow covered cooking, resting time and a pan-juice sauce.",
      "summary": "Bondiola is the pork neck, a cut with fat marbling that turns tender with slow cooking. In Argentina and Uruguay it's a weekend table classic, and in other countries it's sold as pork collar or pork neck. This recipe makes it in a home oven with a marinade of mustard, honey, garlic and soy sauce that caramelizes on the surface.\n\nThe secret is three steps: browning the piece before roasting it, cooking it covered with a little water so it softens, and letting it rest before slicing. The pan juices become a simple sauce to spoon over the slices, served with roast potatoes or chimichurri.",
      "notes": "<p>For a 2 kg piece, start checking tenderness after about 2 hours covered at 160 °C (320 °F). Thickness and ovens change the timing: if still firm, continue covered and keep liquid in the pan. A 1 kg piece will not necessarily take half the time.</p><p>Cover the roasting pan with foil to retain moisture; there is no need to wrap the meat tightly. Uncover only to brown and watch the honey. Whole pork has a safe minimum of 63 °C (145 °F) with 3 minutes of rest; this recipe aims for 80–85 °C for tenderness, also checking with a fork.</p><p>Rest for 15 minutes and slice against the grain. Reheat leftovers with their juices to 74 °C (165 °F) in the center.</p><h3>Storage</h3><p>Refrigerate the meat and juices within 2 hours of taking it out of the oven, in closed containers. It keeps for 3 to 4 days and reheats gently with some of the sauce to 165°F (74°C). You can freeze it in portions for up to 3 months.</p><p class='md-sources'>Sources: <a href='https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/safe-temperature-chart' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>USDA FSIS, minimum safe temperatures</a>; <a href='https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/leftovers-and-food-safety' rel='noopener' target='_blank'>USDA FSIS, leftovers and food safety</a>.</p><p>References: <a href=\"https://www.foodsafety.gov/food-safety-charts/safe-minimum-internal-temperatures\">FoodSafety.gov</a>.</p><h3>More meat recipes for another meal</h3><ul><li><a href=\"/en/creep-stroganoff/\">Beef Stroganoff Crepes with Mushrooms</a></li><li><a href=\"/en/chorizo-santarosano/\">Homemade Santarrosano Chorizo: Fresh Colombian Sausage</a></li></ul>",
      "steps": [
        {
          "title": "Pat dry and season the pork",
          "content": "Take the piece out of the fridge 30 minutes before starting. Pat it dry on all sides with paper towels and season with the salt and pepper. A dry surface browns better and the marinade sticks instead of sliding off.",
          "image_alt": "Raw pork neck patted dry and seasoned on a cutting board",
          "image_url": "/recetas/bondiola-de-cerdo/paso-01.webp"
