    "id": "14f903dc-f571-42fc-8885-516c7625e79f",
    "language": "en",
    "public_path": "/en/colombian-lechona-recipe",
    "recipe_group_id": "d0ad2da1-a1fa-4686-8c83-30303c1c005a",
    "expected_updated_at": "2026-10-21T13:49:01.992102+00:00",
    "before": {
      "title": "Colombian Lechona in the Oven: a Home-Friendly Recipe",
      "excerpt": "Colombian lechona for a home oven: juicy pork, yellow split peas, fragrant rice and crackling skin. With timings and safe temperatures.",
      "summary": "Lechona is one of Colombia's great celebration dishes, closely associated with the Tolima and Huila regions. The traditional version stuffs a whole pig with rice, peas and scallions and cooks it for hours in a wood-fired oven. This recipe adapts it to a home oven: the same flavors, in a deep baking dish, for 8 people.\n\nThree details make it work. The pork rests in the marinade for 8 to 12 hours so salt and spices reach the center. The rice goes into the dish three-quarters cooked and finishes in the pork juices. And the pork belly is baked covered first and uncovered after, with dry skin, so it turns crisp.\n\nYou need to plan ahead: soak the peas and marinate the pork the night before, then allow about 4 hours for cooking and baking. If you are serving it at a celebration, assemble the dish before guests arrive and bake it in the meantime.",
      "notes": "<h3>Tips</h3><ul><li>Crisp skin depends on dryness: do not coat it with the paste, and leave it uncovered in the fridge.</li><li>Cut the shoulder into similar-sized pieces so it cooks evenly.</li><li>The filling touches raw meat while baking: use a thermometer and confirm at least 74 °C (165 °F) in the center before taking it out.</li><li>Do not cook the rice fully before assembling; it would overcook in the dish.</li></ul><h3>Substitutions</h3><ul><li>Lard: vegetable oil works, with less flavor.</li><li>Banana leaves: line the dish with parchment and cover with foil; some aroma is lost.</li><li>Cebolla larga: scallions or spring onions, in the same amount.</li><li>Achiote: sweet paprika, for color.</li><li>Pork belly without skin: the recipe works, but there will be no crackling.</li></ul><h3>Storage</h3><p>Cool leftovers and refrigerate them within 2 hours, in closed containers. They keep for 3 to 4 days and should be reheated until the center reaches 74 °C (165 °F). You can freeze portions for up to 3 months. The skin loses its crunch in storage: revive it for 10 minutes in a 200 °C (400 °F) oven.</p><p class=\"md-sources\">Sources: <a href=\"https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/safe-temperature-chart\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">USDA FSIS, safe minimum internal temperatures</a>; <a href=\"https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/leftovers-and-food-safety\" rel=\"noopener\" target=\"_blank\">USDA FSIS, leftovers and food safety</a>.</p>",
      "steps": [
        {
          "title": "Soak the peas the night before",
          "content": "Put the peas in a bowl and cover them with cold water, about three fingers above the peas. Soak for 8 to 12 hours. The next day, drain and rinse them.",
          "image_alt": "Dried yellow split peas soaking in cold water",
          "image_url": "/recetas/lechona/paso-01.webp"
        },
        {
          "title": "Make the marinade",
          "content": "Mix the scallions, garlic, cumin, pepper, achiote, 1 tablespoon of salt and 2 tablespoons of lard into a thick paste. It should smell intense but balanced; if the garlic dominates, add a little more scallion.",
          "image_alt": "Marinade paste with scallions, garlic, cumin and achiote",
          "image_url": "/recetas/lechona/paso-02.webp"
        },
        {
          "title": "Marinate the pork and let it rest",
          "content": "Rub the paste over the shoulder pieces and over the meat side of the belly; keep the skin clean and dry. Cover the shoulder and refrigerate for 8 to 12 hours. Leave the belly uncovered, skin side up, on the coldest shelf of the fridge: this dries the skin so it crisps later.",
          "image_alt": "Marinated pork shoulder and pork belly with dry skin, ready to chill",
          "image_url": "/recetas/lechona/paso-03.webp"
        },
        {
          "title": "Cook the peas",
          "content": "Put the drained peas in a pot with 1.2 liters of water and ½ teaspoon of salt. Bring to a boil, lower the heat and simmer for 35 to 45 minutes, until tender but still holding their shape. Drain and keep one cup of the cooking liquid.",
          "image_alt": "Yellow split peas simmering gently until tender",
          "image_url": "/recetas/lechona/paso-04.webp"
        },
        {
          "title": "Cook the rice to three-quarters",
          "content": "Sauté the rice for 1 minute in 1 tablespoon of lard. Add 500 ml of water or stock and ½ teaspoon of salt. Bring to a boil, cover and cook over low heat for 8 to 10 minutes, until the liquid is absorbed but the grain is still firm in the center. It will finish cooking in the oven.",
          "image_alt": "Rice cooked to three-quarters, with the grain still firm",
          "image_url": "/recetas/lechona/paso-05.webp"
        },
        {
          "title": "Combine the filling",
          "content": "Mix the rice with the peas, the remaining lard and 3 to 4 tablespoons of the pea cooking liquid. The mixture should be moist, because it will absorb the pork juices. Taste and adjust the salt.",
          "image_alt": "Moist rice and pea filling",
          "image_url": "/recetas/lechona/paso-06.webp"
        },
        {
          "title": "Assemble the dish",
          "content": "Heat the oven to 170 °C (340 °F). Pass the banana leaves over a flame for a few seconds to soften them, and line a deep dish with them, letting the edges hang over. Spread half of the filling, set the pork shoulder pieces with their juices on top and cover with the remaining filling. Place the pork belly on top, skin side up, and sprinkle the coarse salt over the skin.",
          "image_alt": "Dish assembled with banana leaves, filling, pork and belly before baking",
          "image_url": "/recetas/lechona/paso-07.webp"
        },
        {
          "title": "Bake covered for 2 hours",
          "content": "Fold the leaves over the filling and cover the dish with aluminum foil, without letting it touch the skin of the belly. Bake at 170 °C (340 °F) for 2 hours. During this time the pork becomes tender and the rice finishes cooking.",
          "image_alt": "Lechona covered with foil inside the oven",
          "image_url": "/recetas/lechona/paso-08.webp"
        },
        {
          "title": "Uncover and brown until the skin crackles",
          "content": "Remove the foil, raise the oven to 200 °C (400 °F) and bake 45 to 60 minutes more, until the skin is golden and blistered. Use a thermometer to check that the center of the filling reaches at least 74 °C (165 °F) and that the pork falls apart with a fork. If the skin has not puffed up, broil it for 5 to 8 minutes without leaving the oven unattended.",
          "image_alt": "Golden, crackling skin; thermometer in the center of the filling",
          "image_url": "/recetas/lechona/paso-09.webp"
        },
        {
          "title": "Rest, slice and serve",
          "content": "Let the lechona rest for 20 minutes out of the oven. Lift off the belly, cut the crisp skin into pieces and serve a portion of filling with pork and a piece of skin. Serve with arepa and ají.",
          "image_alt": "A portion of lechona with crisp skin, arepa and ají",
          "image_url": "/recetas/lechona/paso-10.webp"
        }
      ],
      "total_time_minutes": 980,
